# Preserve and improve useful abstractions

For code changes, read applicable project instructions and architecture decisions.
Read docs/abstraction-map.md if present, focusing on affected modules. If the map
is missing or stale, inspect real source, callers, and tests. Do not block ordinary
work on the absence of a map, and do not treat proposed entries as approved rules.

Before a substantive edit:
- Identify the module that should own the behavior, its interface, relevant
  callers, and rules that must remain true. State this briefly in the plan.
- Search for existing operations that meet the requirement before adding one.
- Reuse useful abstractions through their public interfaces. Keep business rules
  with their owner; avoid direct internal-state access or duplicated coordination.

When implementing:
- Preserve existing contracts unless the requested change requires a change.
- Shape interfaces around caller tasks. Include relevant inputs, results, failure
  behavior, side effects, ordering, and performance limits in the contract.
- Keep complexity within its owning module when this reduces caller knowledge.
- Do not add pass-through layers, generic frameworks, or speculative extension
  points without a concrete need. Small functions and adapters can still be useful.
- Do not copy a weak pattern merely because it already exists. If an abstraction
  does not fit, explain the gap and make the smallest justified local improvement
  within the task. Avoid unrelated refactors.
- For persistent state and external effects, examine relevant concurrency, retry,
  partial-failure, and consistency rules. Do not assume a transaction covers remote
  services or that atomicity alone ensures correct concurrent behavior.
- Ask only when a material choice is unresolved, such as changed public behavior,
  data compatibility, ownership, or consistency guarantees. Continue unaffected work.
- If changing a contract, trace affected callers and migrate them coherently.
  Preserve compatibility when required; explain any necessary transition.

Before declaring completion:
- Run appropriate existing checks and verify observable behavior through the
  interface. Add focused regression coverage when the changed contract warrants it.
- Review the actual diff for duplicated rules, internal access across modules,
  hidden side effects, unnecessary layers, and affected callers left unchanged.
- Update the relevant abstraction-map entry if ownership or contracts changed.
  Keep observed behavior and proposed design clearly separated.
- Report the behavior changed, modules reused or changed, verification results,
  and any unresolved contract risk. Do not claim checks passed without running them.
